{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-109726",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-14-6,400+225) \\\\  & ∼N(-20,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-20$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{625}=25.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-28<Y\\le 12).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-28<Y\\le 12) & =P(\\frac{-28+20}{25}<Z\\le \\frac{12+20}{25}) \\\\  & =P(-0.32\\le Z\\le 1.28) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.28)-P(Z<-0.32) \\\\  & =Φ(1.28)-Φ(-0.32)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.32) & ≈0.3745 \\\\ Φ(1.28) & ≈0.8997.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-28<Y\\le 12) & =Φ(1.28)-Φ(-0.32) \\\\  & ≈0.8997-0.3745 \\\\  & ≈0.53.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
